			<div class="content" id="post-content-4409419">
									

<p>That's a fair way to feel about it.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Just to explain my thinking a bit (your mileage will vary, of course): The obit is meant not to explain the SCP per se, but to add some additional turns of irony &#8212; not just that the man liked books, not just that he went blind, but that he died trying to restore his eyesight. It's not that the failed surgery, or Bemis's love of books, &quot;caused&quot; the anomaly; honestly that's not my personal interpretation of the anomaly at all.</p>
<p>I can absolutely understand someone thinking the obit's writing style in particular takes things over the top, but for me it added an important layer of not just pathos but also narrative closure. It also brings it closer to its inspiration; it's exactly the sort of note <em>The Twilight Zone</em> would choose to end on with a story like this.</p>
<p>As I said, your mileage may vary. For the things I liked most about this story, I found the obit to be an important tone to add to a story that otherwise necessarily keeps things tight and clinical.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

